WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.762
The final coding exercise asks you to filter JSONB notes.

00:00:03.762 --> 00:00:10.357
The output needs patient ID, symptom, and severity, but only for notes where the follow-up flag is true.

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:14.267
Use the JSONB extraction pattern from the earlier slides.

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:19.872
Think about which values should be returned as text for display and which value needs to be interpreted as a

00:00:19.872 --> 00:00:24.167
Boolean for filtering. Then sort the result by patient ID.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:26.092
Run and Check when you are ready.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:31.082
If the filter returns both true and false notes, review the Boolean comparison.

00:00:31.082 --> 00:00:37.957
If the values display with unexpected JSON formatting, review which JSON extraction operator is being used.

00:00:37.957 --> 00:00:45.232
This exercise combines semi-structured data with the same SELECT and WHERE reasoning you already know.

00:00:45.232 --> 00:00:47.332
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:47.332 --> 00:00:52.387
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:52.387 --> 00:00:57.537
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:57.537 --> 00:01:00.277
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:07.357
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:07.357 --> 00:01:13.727
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:13.727 --> 00:01:16.017
not only whether the statement executes.
